%
% The distinguishability sentence earns its place: the RMS comparison before it
% is about amplitude, and on its own it leaves open whether the ensemble
% collapses to an indistinguishable residual. One sentence answers that. Note
% it got weaker when the ensemble moved into antenna temperature -- PAIR1 was
% 99 per cent when the models were sky-referred -- and it is quoted honestly.
%
% Deliberately absent: the retention-vs-trough-depth correlations (detail; the
% notebook still prints them); the `stays below' rule; and the repeated "N is
% not a filter depth we propose to apply to data", which is block 5's argument
% and is made there, once. EDGES, MIST and REACH are not cited here either:
% the subsection introduces all three a few paragraphs earlier.
%
% ZEUS21 ATTRIBUTION. Its README asks for one paper unconditionally
% (2023MNRAS.523.2587M), a second only if the UVLF module is used, and a third
% for relative velocities, Lyman-Werner feedback or Pop III stars
% (2025PhRvD.111h3503C). Two of the three is correct here: models_21cm's
% generate.py sets USE_POPIII, USE_LW_FEEDBACK and USE_RELATIVE_VELOCITIES,
% and never touches zeus21.UVLF_binned. The README also asks for a link to the
% repository; it is a \footnote at first mention, which is what this manuscript
% does for CROISSANT and every other piece of software.
% ===================================================================

Using a representative beam from our electromagnetic simulations of the EIGSEP
antenna (section~\ref{subsec:eig_antenna}), a model of the horizon profile at
the EIGSEP site (Fig.~\ref{fig:horizon}), and the Global Sky Model
\citep{2017MNRAS.464.3486Z}, we simulated one sidereal day of observation by
EIGSEP over 50--250\,MHz with the forward model described in
section~\ref{subsec:fwd_modelling}.
The simulations of the beam-weighted foregrounds produce predictions for the
antenna temperature measured by EIGSEP.
In the simulations, we assumed a constant ground temperature of 300\,K. This
beam-weighted ground pickup is included in the antenna temperature and not
corrected here.

We then computed the singular value decomposition of the antenna temperature,
producing its orthogonal spectral eigenmodes, and used the resulting
eigenmodes to filter the beam-weighted foregrounds.
In the central panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:singular_values}, we present the residual
RMS after filtering as a function of the number of eigenmodes filtered, $N$.
The first point -- zero modes filtered -- is the RMS of the raw waterfall
pixels about zero. This is approximately @@RMS0@@\,K.
Because the singular value decomposition is not centred, the first mode
captures the mean antenna temperature spectrum.
The residual declines steeply: with one mode filtered, the residual RMS is
@@RMS1@@\,K and with four modes filtered, the residual RMS is @@RMS4@@\,K.
The result shows that the beam-weighted foregrounds occupy a low-dimensional
subspace: filtering $N=@@NAM1@@$ orthogonal modes describes the antenna
temperature to one part in $10^{@@DYNM1@@}$, or @@FGM1@@\,mK.

To assess the spectral overlap between the beam-weighted foregrounds and the
expected cosmological signal, we applied the identical foreground filter to an
ensemble of @@NMODELS@@ global 21-cm models generated with the Zeus21
code\footnote{\url{https://github.com/ZeusCosmo/Zeus21}}
\citep{2023MNRAS.523.2587M, 2025PhRvD.111h3503C}.
Because the eigenmodes are built from the antenna temperature, the models are
first expressed in the same quantity: an isotropic signal is received through
the fraction of the beam that sees open sky, which for our horizon and beam is
@@ETALO@@--@@ETAHI@@ over the band, so the amplitudes quoted below are smaller
than the corresponding sky brightness temperatures by that factor.
The central panel shows that the filter explains foreground variance more
effectively than the cosmological signal, and that after $N=@@NA@@$ modes are
removed, the RMS of the median retained 21-cm signal is larger (@@MED@@\,mK)
than the RMS of the residual foregrounds (@@FG@@\,mK).
What the filter leaves also remains informative: among the @@NDEEP@@ models
with absorption troughs deeper than 50\,mK, filtering reduces the median
separation between a pair of models over 70--130\,MHz from @@SEP0@@ to
@@SEP1@@\,mK, but @@PAIR1@@ per cent of pairs stay separated by more than
1\,mK.


% ===================================================================
% BLOCK 2 -- same section, immediately after block 1. Three paragraphs of
% comparison, then the closing paragraph that used to end block 1.
%
% Paragraph 3 is the destination: the design claim in
% section~\ref{subsec:eig_antenna} is what this block exists to substantiate.
% If the block has to be cut, cut towards that sentence, and keep the two
% framing clauses named in the header.
%
% The closing paragraph reads better here than it did at the end of block 1:
% "knowledge of the sky or the beam may allow some chromatic structure to be
% modelled rather than filtered" lands immediately after three panels
% demonstrating what beam chromaticity costs.
% ===================================================================

The amount of signal that survives this filter is a property of the antenna as
much as of the sky. The three panels of Fig.~\ref{fig:singular_values} repeat
the simulation and the filter for three beams observing the same sky behind the
same horizon: an isotropic beam, which has no chromaticity and so bounds what
any antenna could achieve at this site; the EIGSEP bowtie; and a Vivaldi feed
of the type used for the ground antennas (section~\ref{sec:vivaldis}) and flown
on the first suspension test. Each is filtered in its own eigenbasis and scaled
by its own open-sky fraction, since both are properties of the beam.

The antenna sets both how deep the filter must go and how much signal is left
when it gets there. Filtering the beam-weighted foregrounds to @@LEVEL@@\,mK
takes @@NISO@@ modes for the isotropic beam, @@NBOW@@ for the bowtie and
@@NVIV@@ for the Vivaldi feed; of the unfiltered 21-cm RMS, @@RETISO@@,
@@RETBOW@@ and @@RETVIV@@ per cent respectively remain at that point. Compared
at the same level of foreground suppression, the bowtie therefore retains
@@FACTOR@@ times the signal the Vivaldi feed does, and recovers @@PCTIDEAL@@ per
cent of what an achromatic antenna would. This is not an aperture effect and
runs against one: the Vivaldi feed is the more directive of the two and couples
proportionally more of its response to the sky than to the ground
(@@ETAVIV@@ against @@ETABOW@@), so it begins with more signal and still ends
with less.

This is the design criterion of section~\ref{subsec:eig_antenna} measured
rather than asserted, and it is why the suspended antenna is not simply a
Vivaldi feed of the kind already deployed on the ground. The comparison is
specific to this application: the feed is used here in isolation, without the
dish it was designed to illuminate, and the ground antennas it is used for are
not required to deliver a low-chromaticity global-signal measurement.

While Fig.~\ref{fig:singular_values} is a useful heuristic probing the
covariance between the beam-weighted foregrounds and the 21-cm signal, it does
not represent the complete analysis needed for foreground separation.
Unmodelled instrumental systematics may require more modes filtered than in
this figure.
On the other hand, knowledge of the sky or the beam -- e.g. through beam
mapping (section~\ref{subsec:beam_mapping}) -- may allow some chromatic
structure to be modelled rather than filtered.
In practice, EIGSEP will use a differentiable forward model to evaluate
systematics and separate the foregrounds, as described in
section~\ref{subsec:fwd_modelling}.


